WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.235
Lesson 12.2.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:06.631
Custody architecture: vault, multisig and spending policies.

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:09.241
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:12.285
to design custody so that no single lost device,

00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:15.516
stolen key or mistaken signature can drain the bank.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.106
Here's the plan.

00:00:18.106 --> 00:00:19.791
First, the key ideas.

00:00:19.791 --> 00:00:22.506
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:22.506 --> 00:00:24.031
Then, your checklist.

00:00:24.031 --> 00:00:26.193
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:27.493 --> 00:00:31.055
Scale Module 1's three wallets into a bank-grade

00:00:31.055 --> 00:00:35.489
setup: Smart-account features to use where available: Vault,

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.269
Most of your equity, Multisig, e.g. 2-of-3 hardware

00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:42.913
keys in separate locations, No DeFi approvals.

00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:46.828
Moves only to the Operating tier, to allowlisted addresses.

00:00:46.828 --> 00:00:50.155
Operating, Active positions, Hardware wallet or a

00:00:50.155 --> 00:00:53.848
smart account with limits, Verified protocols only.

00:00:53.848 --> 00:00:55.443
Spending limit per day.

00:00:55.443 --> 00:00:59.668
Hot, Small float, Hot wallet, Anything new or experimental.

00:00:59.668 --> 00:01:00.923
Refilled on schedule.

00:01:02.223 --> 00:01:05.363
Let's look at the diagram: Custody architecture.

00:01:05.363 --> 00:01:06.923
Take a moment with it.

00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:09.333
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:13.148
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:13.148 --> 00:01:16.604
Vault: 2-of-3 multisig, with key A at home, key

00:01:16.604 --> 00:01:19.413
B in a safe-deposit box and key C with

00:01:19.413 --> 00:01:22.953
a trusted person or a professional co-signer.

00:01:22.953 --> 00:01:25.083
Losing any one key is survivable;

00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:27.387
stealing any one key is useless.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.077
Test it: move $10 through

00:01:29.077 --> 00:01:30.767
every path once a quarter.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:33.527
Here's your checklist.

00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:35.062
Do it now, for real.

00:01:35.062 --> 00:01:39.517
First, Vault is multisig; keys in separate physical locations.

00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:42.312
Next, Operating tier has a daily limit.

00:01:42.312 --> 00:01:44.777
Then, Allowlist on vault outflows.

00:01:44.777 --> 00:01:47.802
And finally, Quarterly recovery test done and logged.

00:01:49.102 --> 00:01:50.312
Question 1.

00:01:50.312 --> 00:01:52.597
Why 2-of-3 rather than 1-of-1?

00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:59.611
The answer: One key can be lost or stolen

00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:03.007
without losing the funds or giving an attacker control.

00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:05.552
Question 2.

00:02:05.552 --> 00:02:07.097
What does a timelock buy you?

00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:13.568
The answer: Time to notice and

00:02:13.568 --> 00:02:15.817
cancel an unauthorised large move.

00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:18.387
Question 3.

00:02:18.387 --> 00:02:20.562
Why test recovery with a small amount?

00:02:24.982 --> 00:02:26.963
The answer: A recovery plan you've

00:02:26.963 --> 00:02:28.792
never run is an assumption.

00:02:28.792 --> 00:02:31.072
Testing proves every key and path works.

00:02:32.372 --> 00:02:34.887
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:37.214
Next up, Lesson 12.3: The credit

00:02:37.214 --> 00:02:39.682
line: borrowing like a bank client.
